---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocFigure from '../../../../components/DocFigure.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Expression Limits - MHA Manager"
  description="Per-character ceilings on MetaHuman Animator expression curves, dialled live in the Performance editor and applied to every export."
  currentSlug="expression-limits"
>

  <div class="doc-header">
    <h1 class="doc-title">Expression Limits</h1>
    <p class="doc-lead">
      Some performers push a control further than the character should ever go. Expression Limits
      caps that per character instead of per take: a profile asset holds a floor, a knee and a
      ceiling for each expression, you dial it against a solved take and watch the face follow, and
      every export of every job that names the profile comes out limited. The solved Performance on
      disk is never touched.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Open the panel</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Open a solved Performance asset and click <strong>Expression Limits</strong> in its toolbar.
      With no profile set, <strong>New from Take</strong> creates one next to the Performance, named
      <code>CLP_&lt;Performance&gt;</code>, with one unlimited row per expression the take carries.
      The profile is an ordinary asset: keep it per character and point every job for that character
      at it.
    </p>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/expression-limits.webp"
      alt="The Expression Limits tab docked beside the Performance viewport: face control board on top, one slider row per limited control below, the preview face following as a ceiling is dragged"
      caption="Dialling a limit against a solved take - the viewport follows the slider"
    />
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="el-row header">
        <span>Control</span>
        <span>What it does</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Profile</strong></span>
        <span>Which profile is live on this Performance. The choice is per editor session, not saved into the asset.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Enabled</strong></span>
        <span>Untick to see the raw solve for comparison. Batch exports ignore this switch.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Curves</strong></span>
        <span>Advanced. Swaps the default one-row-per-board-control view for one row per raw expression curve.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Lighting</strong></span>
        <span>On the Performance editor's own toolbar, not this panel. Lights the viewport with one of Epic's MetaHuman rigs (<strong>Studio</strong> by default), a Custom Light Preset registered in MetaHuman Character, or a level from your own lighting folder. The rig's map is streamed into the preview world and unloaded again on switch, so a World Partition level is refused. Remembered for every Performance you open; nothing is written into the asset.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Hair</strong></span>
        <span>Puts the grooms of the MetaHuman whose face mesh the viewport is showing onto the preview face. The character is found from that face mesh, so there is nothing to pick. Remembered like Lighting; nothing is written into the asset.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Schematic</strong></span>
        <span>The real face control board. Knobs sit at the current frame's solved value, red bars mark where each limit cuts the travel. Click a control to add it as a row, drag to move its ceiling, wheel to zoom, right or middle drag to pan.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Rows</strong></span>
        <span>One slider per limited control, with Min / Mid / Max spin boxes, the take's own peak, and a tick at the current frame. Filter by name, or use <strong>Add curve</strong>.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/lighting-toolbar.webp"
      alt="The Lighting combo on the Performance editor toolbar open, listing Preview Scene and Epic's rigs, switching the viewport from Studio to RedLantern"
      caption="The Lighting combo on the Performance toolbar - the rig's map streams into the preview world on switch"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">What a limit does</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Three handles per row. <strong>Mid</strong> is the knee and the one input that passes through
      untouched. Above it the response is scaled so full input lands exactly on <strong>Max</strong>;
      below it the same scaling runs the other way and zero input lands on <strong>Min</strong>. The
      output never leaves Min..Max, and the curve keeps its shape at both ends rather than flattening
      into a plateau. Defaults of 0 / 0.5 / 1 are a no-op.
    </p>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Rows are ordered <code>0 &lt;= Min &lt; Mid &lt; Max &lt;= 1</code>. Dragging one handle past
      another pushes it rather than swapping the two, so a row can never be inverted.
    </p>
    <div class="callout callout-note">
      <strong>Control rows and curve rows are different units.</strong> A board control row spans the
      control's own range, so a two-sided axis such as blink / widen rests at 0.5: its floor limits
      one expression, its ceiling the other, and the knee couples both halves. A curve row limits one
      raw curve over 0..1. Both lists stay live whichever view is showing; control rows apply first.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Live in the Performance editor</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Moving a handle rebakes the take's face animation in place: Sequencer keys, the Control Rig tab
      and the viewport all follow. Long takes rebake on a short delay after you let go rather than on
      every pixel of a drag.
    </p>
    <p class="section-desc">
      The asset on disk stays raw. Limits live in memory only: they are pulled out before the package
      saves and put back afterwards, and a re-solve re-applies them. Epic's own <strong>Export
      Animation</strong> from inside the editor exports the limited curves and stamps the result so it
      is never limited a second time on playback.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">In a batch</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Set <strong>Curve Limit Profile</strong> in a job's Export options, on a Solve Defaults
      template, or per take. Every export that job writes - AnimSequence, Level Sequence Control Rig
      bake, and FBX - comes out limited; leaving it empty means no limits. The frames are limited for
      the length of the export and restored afterwards, so <strong>Re-Export</strong> with an edited
      profile regenerates every output without re-solving.
    </p>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Each job logs what its limits actually did: per curve, the take's own peak, what that peak
      became, and the share of frames that changed. The same report rides in the saved manifest. A
      limit that reports no affected frames is a limit that was never reached.
    </p>
    <div class="callout callout-note">
      A Performance open in the editor with a profile live steps aside for the length of a batch
      export, so a queued job always exports from the raw solve and never limits twice.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Limits on a character, outside a take</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Right-click the profile asset in the Content Browser:
    </p>
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="el-row header">
        <span>Action</span>
        <span>What it does</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Preview Curve Limits</strong></span>
        <span>Plays the profile's range-of-motion animation on its preview face mesh with the limits inserted, so you can judge them without a take.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Apply to Character...</strong></span>
        <span>Points a face mesh at a limited copy of its post-process AnimBlueprint, so everything that character plays back in the editor is limited.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="el-row">
        <span><strong>Remove from Characters</strong></span>
        <span>Restores every mesh the profile was applied to, exactly as it was.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout callout-warn">
      <strong>Apply to Character blocks cooking.</strong> The limited AnimBlueprint is editor-only
      workflow content, so an applied mesh will not cook until Remove from Characters is run. The
      confirmation dialog says so before it changes anything.
    </div>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .doc-header { margin-bottom: 40px; }

  .doc-title {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .doc-lead {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 620px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .doc-lead strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .doc-section { margin-bottom: 48px; }

  .section-title {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .section-desc {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .section-desc strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .field-list {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .el-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 3fr;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 11px 16px;
    font-size: 0.825rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    align-items: start;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .el-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .el-row.header {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .el-row strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .callout {
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-top: 16px;
    border-left: 3px solid;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .callout-warn { background: color-mix(in srgb, #f0a500 8%, transparent); border-color: #f0a500; }
  .callout-note { background: var(--bg-surface); border-color: var(--border); }
  .callout strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }

  a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .el-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
  }
</style>
